        'pairs' => [
            'TH_TF' => 'Your answers suggest hurt or a need for comfort when things get tough, and quicker, more intense feelings when things become especially difficult. These can be different ways of responding to demands; feeling fired up does not necessarily mean hurt caused it or is hidden underneath. Notice which feelings are present, what matters in the situation and whether you need care, space or time to consider a response. Sensitivity and energy can both be strengths. Understanding how they appear for you can help you choose what fits.',
            'TH_BS' => 'Your answers suggest feeling hurt or needing comfort when things get tough, alongside a tendency to pull back when demands become especially difficult. Space may help you gather yourself, while at other times it may make care or connection harder to reach. This does not establish that withdrawing is shielding hidden feelings. Notice what happens around a pause and whether you feel more rested, clearer or further from what matters afterwards. You can respect your need for privacy while considering a manageable response to what you need.',
            'TH_BP' => 'Your answers suggest hurt or a need for care when things get tough, and more effort or busyness when demands become especially difficult. Continuing can help you meet responsibilities, but it may also leave less room to notice how you are feeling. The two descriptions do not prove that work is covering up hurt. Notice what persistence gives you in the situation and whether care, rest or support also deserves some space. Being sensitive and dependable can remain strengths without every demand taking priority over your needs.',
            'TH_A' => 'Your answers suggest feeling hurt or needing comfort when things get tough, and an ability to slow down and consider what you need when things become especially difficult. These responses can sit alongside one another. Reflection does not have to replace the feeling, and hurt does not have to become overwhelming before you can use it. You might acknowledge what matters to you, then consider a workable next step. That could involve care, some space, a practical adjustment or support where it is available and safe.',
            'TF_TH' => 'Your answers suggest quick, intense feelings when things get tough, and hurt or a stronger need for comfort when things become especially difficult. This does not mean frustration is hiding sadness or that one feeling must turn into the other. Notice which feelings you recognise and what is happening around them. Energy may help you speak up or act, while sensitivity may draw attention to something important. A pause can give you room to consider both the feeling and what kind of care or response would fit.',
            'TF_BS' => 'Your answers suggest quick, intense feelings when things get tough, alongside a tendency to become quieter or pull back when demands become especially difficult. These responses may occur in different situations rather than as a reaction followed by a shutdown. Notice whether space helps you settle and choose what comes next, or leaves you disconnected from what matters. You can make room for energy and quiet without treating either as a failure. A manageable pause may help you decide whether to respond, rest or return to something later.',
            'TF_BP' => 'Your answers suggest energy or quick reactions when things get tough, and a tendency to work harder or hold things more tightly when demands become especially difficult. Acting and persevering can both be useful, although either may narrow your room to pause. The results do not establish that extra effort is correcting an earlier mistake or controlling an unacceptable feeling. Notice the demands, the energy you have available and what you are trying to achieve. That can help you choose between acting, adjusting the standard, sharing a responsibility or pausing.',
            'TF_A' => 'Your answers suggest quick, intense feelings when things get tough, and a reflective response when things become especially difficult. Energy can help you engage with what matters, while a pause can help you consider how to use it. Reflection is not only available after something goes wrong. Notice opportunities to consider your options while the feeling is present. If you decide that a repair is needed, choose a manageable and safe way to make it; direct contact with someone unsafe or unavailable is not required.',
            'BS_TH' => 'Your answers suggest pulling back when things get tough, and hurt or a stronger need for care when things become especially difficult. These descriptions do not establish that feelings have been stored away or must eventually break through. Quiet may be useful, and a need for comfort may also deserve attention. Notice whether time apart leaves you rested or makes your needs harder to recognise. You can take a manageable step towards care, a familiar helpful activity or safe support while respecting your need for privacy.',
            'BS_TF' => 'Your answers suggest becoming quieter or keeping some distance when things get tough, and quicker, more intense feelings when demands become especially difficult. That does not mean quiet has caused pressure to build until it bursts out. Notice the circumstances around each response, including what has changed and what you need. Space can be useful when it helps you settle; energy can be useful when it helps you act deliberately. A brief check-in may help you choose how much distance or engagement fits the moment.',
            'BS_BP' => 'Your answers suggest two Bodyguard responses: taking some distance when things get tough, and doing more when demands become especially difficult. Both may help you manage a situation, but they do not necessarily serve the same purpose or happen in a fixed order. Notice whether quiet is restoring you and whether effort is moving something useful forward. If either leaves little room for your needs, consider a manageable adjustment. You can keep the strengths in independence and persistence while making space to rest, review a responsibility or ask for safe support.',
            'BS_A' => 'Your answers suggest pulling back when things get tough, and slowing down to consider what you need when things become especially difficult. Reflection does not have to mean giving up quiet time, and quiet does not necessarily mean avoiding something. Notice whether a pause gives you useful space and helps you see your options. You may be able to combine privacy with a practical next step, rest or support that fits your circumstances. The aim is a choice that works for you, rather than forcing engagement.',
            'BP_TH' => 'Your answers suggest continuing with effort and responsibility when things get tough, and hurt or a stronger need for comfort when demands become especially difficult. This does not establish that the feeling has been waiting for exhaustion or a crash. Notice what is asking for your attention and whether your own need for care has room alongside it. Persistence can remain a strength while you make a manageable adjustment, acknowledge a feeling or use a helpful activity. Safe support is one option when it is available.',
            'BP_TF' => 'Your answers suggest working hard to keep things moving when things get tough, and quicker, more intense feelings when demands become especially difficult. These descriptions do not mean pressure must build until you snap. Notice effort, urgency and frustration in their actual context. They may tell you something about the demands you are facing or the response you want to make. A pause, a change of standard or a shared responsibility can create room to consider your next step without losing the useful drive in your persistence.',
            'BP_BS' => 'Your answers suggest two Bodyguard responses: continuing with effort when things get tough, and pulling back when demands become especially difficult. That does not mean you must go from doing everything to doing nothing. Notice what each response offers in the situation. Effort may help you meet a responsibility, while quiet may create room to recover or think. You can consider a manageable pace, a boundary or some support before deciding what to continue and what to put down. Rest does not have to wait until you have nothing left.',
            'BP_A' => 'Your answers suggest persistence and responsibility when things get tough, and a more reflective response when demands become especially difficult. The Adult does not have to arrive late or wait for the Bodyguard to wear itself out. You can notice the strength in your effort while considering what the situation needs and what is workable for you. A brief pause may help you decide what to continue, share, simplify or leave for later. Reflection can support your persistence rather than replace it.',
            'AS_TH' => 'Your answers suggest that you often use perspective and planning when things get tough, while current demands may be stretching your capacity. When things become especially difficult, you also recognise hurt or a need for comfort. That does not mean your coping has failed or that carrying a load caused the feeling. Notice what needs attention in the situation and what care would be useful. You can draw on existing skills while making room for rest, a manageable change or support where it is available and safe.',
            'AS_TF' => 'Your answers suggest reflective coping when things get tough, alongside demands that may be stretching your capacity. You also recognise quicker, more intense feelings when things become especially difficult. These do not prove that you have lost control or that other people must notice a change before you do. Notice urgency, energy or frustration in context, then consider which response would fit. A pause or a practical adjustment may help you protect your capacity while still making room for what matters to you.',
            'AS_BS' => 'Your answers suggest that you often work things through when things get tough, while the current load may be stretching your capacity. You also recognise a tendency to pull back when demands become especially difficult. Some space may be exactly what you need; at other times it may make useful activities or support harder to reach. Notice the effect of a pause rather than judging quiet by itself. You can protect your privacy while choosing a manageable way to rest, adjust a demand or reconnect with something helpful.',
            'AS_BP' => 'Your answers suggest perspective and planning when things get tough, alongside demands that may be stretching your capacity. You also recognise doing more or holding to high standards when things become especially difficult. That can help with a responsibility, and it can also reduce the room available for rest or adjustment. Notice when an extra push is a deliberate, useful choice and when another approach would fit better. Your existing skills can help you review a standard, share a task or protect time for your needs.',
            'AD_TH' => 'Your answers suggest you often respond with perspective and flexibility when things get tough. They also support hurt or a need for care in especially difficult situations. This does not mean there is a hidden weakness or that the second response predicts what will happen next. You can value your steadiness while making room for feelings that deserve attention. Notice what matters in the situation and which forms of care help. Existing routines, a familiar activity or safe support may all be useful, depending on your circumstances.',
            'AD_TF' => 'Your answers suggest you often stay reflective when things get tough, and also recognise quicker, more intense feelings in especially difficult situations. Being steady does not mean you should never feel urgent, frustrated or excited. These responses can offer information about what matters, without deciding what you must do. Notice whether the energy helps you act deliberately or whether a pause would create more choice. You can build on existing strengths and adjust to the situation without treating a difficult moment as a failure.',
            'AD_BS' => 'Your answers suggest perspective and flexible coping when things get tough, alongside a tendency to pull back in especially difficult situations. Quiet may be restorative or may make it harder to stay connected with what helps; the result alone cannot decide that for you. Notice what happens around a pause and how you feel afterwards. You can maintain useful routines and respect a need for privacy while considering a manageable next step. There is no need to manufacture a problem when your current approach is working.',
            'AD_BP' => 'Your answers suggest reflective, flexible coping when things get tough, and also recognise a tendency to do more or hold to high standards in especially difficult situations. Effort is not automatically a problem, and this pairing does not predict that you will become exhausted. Notice whether persistence fits the demands and leaves room for your needs. You can draw on your existing perspective to adjust a standard, protect rest or share a responsibility when useful. The aim is to keep useful choices available as circumstances change.',
        ],
    ],
    's5' => [
        'opening' => 'This section reflects how you rated five statements about the way you tend to see things. Your answers describe your experience; they do not tell us why it developed or define who you are.',
        'high_sentence' => 'The statements you rated quite like you or very like you were {list}.',
        'statements' => [
            '3.1' => [
                'title' => 'Worrying people will leave, or that something is wrong with you',
                'statement' => 'Deep down I worry people will let me down or leave, or that there\'s something wrong with me.',
                'high' => [
                    'title' => 'Feeling not quite enough',
                    'paras' => [
                        'You said this is [rating label]. Worrying that people may leave, let you down or see something wrong with you can make closeness feel less secure. The worry itself deserves attention; it does not establish that someone will leave or that there is something wrong with you. Your answer does not tell us where the worry began.',
                        'You might notice yourself looking for reassurance, watching for signs of distance or holding back. Those are possibilities to consider, rather than things this result says you do. Notice what is happening now, what you know about the situation and what remains uncertain. A manageable step towards care or safe support can help you respond to the worry without treating it as proof.',
                    ],
                ],
                'mid' => [
                    'title' => 'A little worry about being enough',
                    'paras' => [
                        'You said this is [rating label]. Some worry about whether people will stay, or whether you measure up, can become more noticeable in particular situations. Notice when it appears and what helps you feel supported. You can take the feeling seriously while checking whether the situation supports the conclusion you are worried about.',
                    ],
                ],
                'fragment' => 'worrying that people will leave, or that something is wrong with you',
            ],
            '3.2' => [
                'title' => 'Doubting you can handle things on your own',
                'statement' => 'I often doubt I can handle things on my own, or feel like I\'ll end up failing.',
                'high' => [
                    'title' => 'Doubting yourself',
                    'paras' => [
                        'You said this is [rating label]. Doubting that you can handle things, or expecting to fail, can make starting or deciding feel difficult. This answer describes how you see your ability in those moments. It does not establish your actual ability, how much help the task requires or how this doubt developed.',
                        'You might look for reassurance, delay a decision or hesitate to try something new. Check whether those examples fit your experience. Consider what the task asks of you, what you have managed before and what information or practical support would help. A manageable step can give you something concrete to review; asking for help can also be a sensible response to a demanding situation.',
                    ],
                ],
                'mid' => [
                    'title' => 'Some self-doubt',
                    'paras' => [
                        'You said this is [rating label]. Self-doubt may be stronger in some situations than others. Notice the demands, your available energy and what would make the next step clearer. Looking at a specific example can help you recognise both the difficulty and the skills or support you can draw on.',
                    ],
                ],
                'fragment' => 'doubting you can handle things on your own',
            ],
            '3.3' => [
                'title' => 'Finding it hard to make yourself do things',
                'statement' => 'When I don\'t feel like doing something, I find it really hard to make myself do it anyway.',
                'high' => [
                    'title' => 'When it\'s hard to get started',
                    'paras' => [
                        'You said this is [rating label]. You recognise a gap between intending to do something and getting started when you do not feel like doing it. Notice which tasks are harder to begin and what is happening around them. This answer alone cannot tell us whether the difficulty involves tiredness, competing demands, uncertainty about the task or something else.',
                        'If a task keeps getting put off, look at what would make its first step more workable. That might mean making the step smaller, clarifying what is needed or choosing a more suitable time. Check what happens when you try an adjustment. Difficulty starting does not, by itself, tell us anything about your character or establish why it is happening.',
                    ],
                ],
                'mid' => [
                    'title' => 'Sometimes hard to get going',
                    'paras' => [
                        'You said this is [rating label]. Notice when getting started is difficult and when it is easier. A small first step or a clear cue may make a particular task more manageable. Use what you notice to adjust the task or timing rather than assuming the same approach will work every time.',
                    ],
                ],
                'fragment' => 'struggling to make yourself do things',
            ],
            '3.4' => [
                'title' => 'Putting everyone else first',
                'statement' => 'I put everyone else\'s needs ahead of my own, even when it wears me down.',
                'high' => [
                    'title' => 'Putting others first',
                    'paras' => [
                        'You said this is [rating label]. Caring for others can be a strength, and you recognise that your own needs can come behind theirs even when it wears you down. That answer deserves attention without assuming why the pattern developed, who relies on you or what others expect from you.',
                        'Notice where your needs are being squeezed out and what flexibility you actually have. A manageable adjustment might involve protecting a pause, changing a commitment or seeking practical support. What is workable depends on your responsibilities and safety. If a relationship or situation feels unsafe, do not treat direct discussion or setting a boundary with that person as a required step.',
                    ],
                ],
                'mid' => [
                    'title' => 'Often putting others first',
                    'paras' => [
                        'You said this is [rating label]. Caring about other people\'s needs is a strength. Notice the situations where your own needs move down the list and whether that is costing you rest or support. Consider a manageable adjustment that fits your responsibilities and safety.',
                    ],
                ],
                'fragment' => 'putting everyone else\'s needs ahead of your own',
            ],
            '3.5' => [
                'title' => 'High standards and finding it hard to switch off',
                'statement' => 'I hold myself to very high standards, and I find it hard to switch off or relax.',
                'high' => [
                    'title' => 'High standards and switching off',
                    'paras' => [
                        'You said this is [rating label]. High standards can help you do careful work, and you also recognise difficulty switching off or relaxing. Notice when the standard is useful and when it keeps asking for more after the task has met its purpose. Your answer does not establish how those standards developed.',
                        'You might recognise extra checking, difficulty leaving something unfinished or a sense that rest has to wait. Check which, if any, fit your experience. Consider what the task actually requires and where a clear stopping point would help. This answer alone does not establish a sleep problem or predict exhaustion.',
                    ],
                ],
                'mid' => [
                    'title' => 'Fairly high standards',
                    'paras' => [
                        'You said this is [rating label]. Caring about doing things well can be a strength. Notice when effort fits the task and when it starts to take time from rest or other needs. A clearer stopping point or a realistic standard may help in a particular situation; check what works for you.',
                    ],
                ],
                'fragment' => 'holding yourself to very high standards and struggling to switch off',
            ],
        ],
        'less_heading' => 'What is less like you',
        'zeros' => 'You rated the following as not like you: {list}.',
        'ones' => 'You rated the following as a little like you: {list}.',
        'labels' => [
            0 => 'not like me',
            1 => 'a little like me',
            2 => 'somewhat like me',
            3 => 'quite like me',
            4 => 'very like me',
        ],
    ],
    's6' => [
        'opening' => 'When things get tough, you told us you tend to {q41}. You rated how these ways of coping are working for you as {q43}.',
        'context42' => 'When things become especially difficult, you selected {q42}.',
        'helpful' => [
            'low' => 'Your rating is towards the helpful end of the scale. Notice which responses are useful, in what situations and what you value about them. You can build on what is working while staying aware of any costs or changes over time. Different situations may call for different responses, so it can help to have more than one way to meet your needs.',
            'mid' => 'You placed your rating in the middle of the scale. It may be useful to look at each response separately: what it offers in the moment, how you feel afterwards and whether the effect changes with the situation. Keep what you find useful and consider an adjustment where a response creates difficulties. Your rating is a starting point for that reflection; it does not mean every way of coping has the same effect.',
            'high' => 'Your rating is towards the end of the scale marked “it makes things worse.” That deserves attention. Notice which responses seem to add difficulty and whether any still help in particular situations. Choose one manageable adjustment to explore, or seek support suited to what is happening. You can review the effect over time rather than trying to change everything at once. This rating reflects your overall experience; it does not mean every response you selected is harmful.',
        ],
        'coping' => [
            '4.1.a' => 'Keeping busy can help you meet responsibilities and keep things moving when life is difficult. Notice whether the effort is useful in the situation and whether it leaves room to recognise what you need. If you are continuing while tired or stretched, consider a manageable adjustment, such as a clear stopping point, a pause or a change in priorities. Persistence can remain a strength while you make room for rest and support that fits your circumstances.',
            '4.1.b' => 'Going quiet can give you space to rest, gather your thoughts or reduce demands for a while. Notice how you feel afterwards and whether the space helps you respond to what you need. If pulling back makes useful activities or safe support harder to reach, consider a manageable way to reconnect. That might mean returning to a familiar activity or making contact when it is safe and helpful. Respecting your need for privacy can sit alongside staying connected with what matters to you.',
            '4.1.c' => 'Distraction can offer a break from difficult feelings or give you room to get through a demanding moment. Notice what kind of distraction you choose, how long you use it and how you feel afterwards. If it leaves something important unattended or makes it harder to return to what matters, consider a clearer stopping point or another way to respond. A useful distraction can sit alongside rest, practical action or an activity that helps you settle.',
            '4.1.d' => 'When things are difficult, you may notice criticism directed at yourself. Pay attention to the words you use and whether they help you understand what happened or make the situation harder to face. Try describing the difficulty in specific terms, including what you can influence and what support or adjustment would help. A fairer response to yourself can still include responsibility, learning and a practical next step.',
            '4.1.e' => 'Reaching out can give you space to be heard, think something through or get practical help. Notice who feels safe to approach and what kind of response is useful to you. When support is available, being clear about what you need can make the conversation easier, whether that is listening, encouragement or help with a task. What matters is whether the contact supports you; you can choose what to share and with whom.',
            '4.1.f' => 'You selected trying to numb things out when life gets difficult. Notice what you turn to, what you hope it will do and how you feel afterwards. Consider whether it gives you a useful break or creates difficulties of its own. If it is becoming hard to control or affecting your wellbeing, seek support that fits what is happening. A familiar activity that helps you settle may offer another way to get through a difficult moment.',
            '4.1.g' => 'Thinking things through can help you understand a situation and consider your options. Notice whether the thinking is bringing useful information or a workable next step, or whether you are returning to the same questions without getting clearer. If you feel stuck in a loop, try noting what you know, what remains uncertain and one thing you can act on. Some questions may need more information, time or support rather than another round of analysis.',
            '4.1.h' => 'You recognise that your responses to other people can become short or sharp when things are difficult. Notice what is happening around those moments and the effect of your words or actions. A brief pause may give you room to choose how to respond. If you decide a repair is needed, consider a safe and appropriate way to acknowledge the impact and what you would like to do differently. Understanding the situation can support change while keeping responsibility for your response in view.',
            '4.1.i' => 'Acting quickly can be useful when a situation calls for a prompt response. At other times, it can leave less room to consider the consequences or what matters to you. Notice when speed helps and when a pause would give you more choice. Where there is time, take a moment to check what you are about to do, what could follow and whether it fits your intentions. The aim is to make a deliberate response that suits the situation.',
            '4.1.j' => 'Slowing down can give you room to notice how you are feeling, understand the demands in front of you and consider what you need. Notice what makes that pause useful and when it is easier to make space for it. You can build on this response by choosing a manageable next step, whether that involves rest, a practical adjustment or support that fits your circumstances. Taking time to reflect can be a strength, while some situations also call for a timely decision.',
            '4.1.k' => 'Comfort or reassurance can help you feel cared for when things are difficult or uncertain. Notice what you are seeking and whether the response helps. Comfort might come from a familiar activity, a calming routine or safe support when it is available. If reassurance brings only brief relief and you find yourself needing the same answer again, pause to consider what remains uncertain and what would help you respond to it. You can value care from others while developing ways to care for yourself.',
            '4.1.l' => 'Wanting to get things right can support careful work, and you recognise that you put pressure on yourself even when things are difficult. Notice what the task actually requires and whether the standard you are aiming for leaves room for your needs. If checking or extra effort continues after the task has met its purpose, try setting a clear stopping point or defining what good enough means for this situation. Care and commitment can remain strengths while you allow room for rest and adjustment.',
        ],
        'closing' => [
            'TH' => 'When feelings are strong, try a small act of care for yourself. Acknowledge what you are feeling and choose a manageable response to what you need.',
            'TF' => 'When feelings are intense and the situation allows, take a brief pause before responding. Use that moment to notice what matters and choose a response that fits your intentions.',
            'BS' => 'Take a brief, gentle check-in with yourself. Notice how you are feeling, whether quiet is helping and what you need next. Choose a manageable step while respecting your need for space.',
            'BP' => 'Build a brief pause into the effort you are making. Check what needs your attention, what can wait and whether your own needs have room alongside your responsibilities. Use that check to choose a workable next step.',
            'AS' => 'Make room for rest alongside the demands you are managing. Notice where an adjustment, a clearer limit or practical support could ease the load. Use your existing skills to choose a next step that fits the capacity you have today.',
            'AD' => 'Keep making space for the routines and responses you find useful. Notice what helps them fit into everyday life, and adjust them when your needs or circumstances change. Building on what is working can give you familiar options to draw on during more demanding times.',
            'neutral' => 'Use what you have noticed about your responses to choose one manageable next step. Build on what helps, and consider an adjustment where something is adding difficulty. Let your needs, circumstances and safety guide what you try.',
        ],
    ],
    's7' => [
        'opening' => 'You told us that {areas} {isare} connected to how you have been feeling.',
        'impact' => 'You rated the effect on your day-to-day life as {n} out of 10.',
        'recent' => 'You said some of this started or happened in the last six months. A recent change can take attention and adjustment. Notice what you need now, without assuming how quickly your feelings or circumstances should change.',
        'impact_high' => 'You rated the interference towards the higher end of the scale. That deserves attention, including whether practical or professional support would be useful alongside this wellbeing programme.',
        'impact_mid' => 'You rated an effect on your everyday life. Consider which part is asking most of you and what would make a manageable difference.',
        'impact_low' => 'You rated the interference towards the lower end of the scale. You can recognise what is working while considering any area you would still like to understand or support.',
        'areas' => [
            '1.4.a' => [
                'heading' => 'Work or study',
                'text' => 'Pressure, conflict or change at work or study can affect how you feel and what you have available for the rest of life. Consider the demands and the practical conditions around them. If an Employee Assistance Program or student support service is available, check what it offers, who can use it and its privacy arrangements.',
            ],
            '1.4.b' => [
                'heading' => 'Money',
                'text' => 'Money concerns can take time and attention. A practical source of help is the National Debt Helpline, which offers free, confidential financial counselling on 1800 007 007. Its website explains current contact options. This wellbeing report does not provide personal financial advice.',
            ],
            '1.4.c' => [
                'heading' => 'Relationships or family',
                'text' => 'Conflict, separation or changes in a relationship can affect other parts of life. Relationships Australia provides information and a way to find relevant services. The appropriate support depends on your situation. If you report feeling unsafe at home or in a relationship, the separate specialist-support pathway takes priority; this report will not recommend confronting someone, repairing that relationship or attending joint counselling.',
            ],
            '1.4.d' => [
                'heading' => 'Health',
                'text' => 'Your health, or the health of someone close to you, can affect everyday demands and wellbeing. A GP or your existing health professional can help you consider what assessment or support is appropriate. Eligibility and costs for mental health care depend on your circumstances; this report does not determine them.',
            ],
            '1.4.e' => [
                'heading' => 'Loss or grief',
                'text' => 'Grief can have different meanings and time courses. This programme does not set a deadline for how you should feel. Griefline is now part of SANE Australia; its website explains current grief resources and support routes. Its phone number, 1300 845 745, now connects to SANE service enquiries. Check the provider’s current arrangements rather than treating it as a crisis line.',
            ],
            '1.4.f' => [
                'heading' => 'Loneliness or feeling cut off from people',
                'text' => 'Feeling cut off can matter even when other parts of life are going well. Recognising a need for connection does not mean something is wrong with you or that a personal support network is available. The unavailable-support branch offers independent responses and realistic professional-support references without making personal contact a task.',
            ],
            '1.4.g' => [
                'heading' => 'Becoming a parent or caring for someone who is unwell',
                'text' => 'Parenting or caring can bring meaningful responsibilities alongside demands on time, energy and rest. Your own needs deserve a place in that picture. Carer Gateway offers information and support for eligible unpaid carers on 1800 422 737. Check what fits your role rather than assuming every parent or carer is eligible for the same service.',
            ],
            '1.4.h' => [
                'heading' => 'Moving house or city or another big change',
                'text' => 'A change can affect routines, relationships, responsibilities and the sense of what is familiar. Notice which part needs practical attention and what remains useful. This report does not assume that feeling unsettled means the change was wrong or that a particular recovery time applies.',
            ],
            '1.4.i' => [
                'heading' => 'An accident or a frightening event',
                'text' => 'A frightening event may affect how you feel afterwards. This answer alone does not establish a diagnosis or that the danger has ended. If the effects concern you or interfere with life, consider appropriate professional support. Immediate safety needs remain separate from a self-guided wellbeing activity.',
            ],
            '1.4.j' => [
                'heading' => 'Alcohol or other substances',
                'text' => 'If alcohol or another substance is part of the picture, consider its effects in your actual circumstances without assuming why use began. The National Alcohol and Other Drug Hotline provides information and support on 1800 250 015 and directs callers to state or territory services. Check current local availability; the provider lists different service hours for South Australia.',
            ],
        ],
    ],
    's8' => [
        'opening' => 'When you are struggling, you selected {prefs} as useful responses from other people. These preferences can help you recognise what fits when support is available and safe. They do not establish that a particular person can provide it.',
        'prefs' => [
            '5.1.a' => [
                'heading' => 'Having someone just listen',
                'text' => 'Being heard may be useful without immediately receiving a solution. Where a person is available and safe, consider whether you want listening, practical help or both. A preference for listening does not mean you never want advice.',
            ],
            '5.1.b' => [
                'heading' => 'Being given practical steps',
                'text' => 'You selected practical steps as useful support. Clear information or help with one manageable part of a difficulty may fit. Notice whether the steps are relevant, realistic and within your capacity.',
            ],
            '5.1.c' => [
                'heading' => 'Being left to work it out alone',
                'text' => 'You selected space to work things out as useful. Notice when privacy helps you think and what practical demands remain. Space can be respected without requiring a promise to return to a conversation or treating every quiet period as a problem.',
            ],
            '5.1.d' => [
                'heading' => 'Gentle encouragement',
                'text' => 'You selected gentle encouragement as useful. Notice what kind of encouragement helps and whether it respects the difficulty you are facing. Encouragement should not become pressure to act before an option fits.',
            ],
            '5.1.e' => [
                'heading' => 'Being checked in on',
